\label{ch:cfg-ir}

The control-flow graph (CFG) is the foundational data structure for program analysis and optimization. It represents the flow of control through a program as a directed graph, where nodes are basic blocks (sequences of instructions with a single entry and exit) and edges represent possible transfers of control. This chapter develops the theory and practice of CFGs, from construction to analysis.

%% ============================================================
%% Section 55.1
%% ============================================================
\section{Basic Blocks and Edges}\label{sec:basic-blocks}

A \emph{basic block} is a maximal sequence of instructions that is always executed linearly: control enters at the beginning and leaves at the end, with no branches in or out.

\subsection{Definition}\label{sec:bb-def}

\begin{definition}[Basic Block]\label{def:basic-block}
A \emph{basic block} is a sequence of instructions $i_1, i_2, \ldots, i_n$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Control can only enter the block at $i_1$ (no branches into the middle).
  \item Control leaves only after executing $i_n$ (no branches out of the middle).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Block Boundaries}\label{sec:block-boundaries}

A new basic block begins at:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The first instruction of the program.
  \item Any instruction that is the target of a branch.
  \item Any instruction immediately following a branch or jump.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Edges}\label{sec:cfg-edges}

Edges in a CFG represent possible transfers of control:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Unconditional edges.} From a block ending in an unconditional jump to the target block.
  \item \textbf{Conditional edges.} From a block ending in a conditional branch to both the true and false targets.
  \item \textbf{Fall-through edges.} From a block to the immediately following block (when there is no explicit jump).
\end{itemize}

