\section{Exceptional Control Flow}\label{sec:exceptional-cfg}

Exceptions introduce additional control flow edges that are often implicit.

\subsection{Exception Edges}\label{sec:exception-edges}

Any instruction that may throw an exception has an implicit edge to an exception handler:

\begin{verbatim}
  // B1: x = a / b  (may throw DivisionByZero)
  // Implicit edge to exception handler block
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Try-Catch-Finally}\label{sec:try-catch}

Try-catch-finally constructs create complex CFG structures:

\begin{verbatim}
  try {
    // B1: may throw
  } catch (E) {
    // B2: handler
  } finally {
    // B3: cleanup (always executed)
  }
  // B4: continuation

  // CFG edges:
  // B1 -> B2 (exception)
  // B1 -> B3 (normal)
  // B2 -> B3 (after handler)
  // B3 -> B4 (after cleanup)
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Impact on Analysis}\label{sec:exception-analysis}

Exception edges complicate dataflow analysis because they create additional paths through the CFG. Optimizations that are safe for normal control flow may be unsafe in the presence of exceptions.

