\section{CFG Properties: Reachability, Dominance}\label{sec:cfg-properties}

The CFG has several important structural properties that underpin program analysis.

\subsection{Reachability}\label{sec:reachability}

\begin{definition}[Reachability]\label{def:reachability}
A block $B$ is \emph{reachable} from block $A$ if there is a path from $A$ to $B$ in the CFG. A block is \emph{reachable} if it is reachable from the entry block.
\end{definition}

Unreachable blocks (dead code) can be removed without affecting program behavior.

\subsection{Dominance}\label{sec:dominance}

\begin{definition}[Dominance]\label{def:dominance}
Block $D$ \emph{dominates} block $N$ (written $D \;\mathrm{dom}\; N$) if every path from the entry to $N$ must pass through $D$. The entry block dominates all blocks.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Immediate Dominator]\label{def:idom}
The \emph{immediate dominator} of $N$ (written $\mathrm{idom}(N)$) is the unique block $D$ that strictly dominates $N$ but does not strictly dominate any other block that strictly dominates $N$.
\end{definition}

The dominance relation forms a tree (the \emph{dominator tree}), where the parent of each node is its immediate dominator.

