\subsection{Dominance Frontiers}\label{sec:dominance-frontiers}

\begin{definition}[Dominance Frontier]\label{def:dominance-frontier}
The \emph{dominance frontier} of a block $D$ is the set of blocks $N$ such that $D$ dominates a predecessor of $N$ but does not strictly dominate $N$:
\[
  \mathrm{DF}(D) = \{N \mid \exists P \in \mathrm{pred}(N).\, D \;\mathrm{dom}\; P \text{ and } D \not\mathrm{sdom}\; N\}
\]
\end{definition}

Dominance frontiers are used to place phi functions in SSA construction (see \cref{ch:ssa}).

\subsection{Computing Dominators}\label{sec:computing-dominators}

The classic algorithm for computing dominators is iterative dataflow:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Iterative Dominator Computation}\label{alg:dominators}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{ComputeDominators}{$\mathrm{CFG}$}
  \State $\mathrm{dom}(\mathrm{entry}) \gets \{\mathrm{entry}\}$
  \For{all blocks $B \neq \mathrm{entry}$}
    \State $\mathrm{dom}(B) \gets \mathrm{AllBlocks}$
  \EndFor
  \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{true}$
  \While{$\mathrm{changed}$}
    \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{false}$
    \For{all blocks $B \neq \mathrm{entry}$}
      \Let{\mathrm{new\_dom}}{\{B\} \cup \bigcap_{P \in \mathrm{pred}(B)} \mathrm{dom}(P)}
      \If{$\mathrm{new\_dom} \neq \mathrm{dom}(B)$}
        \State $\mathrm{dom}(B) \gets \mathrm{new\_dom}$
        \State $\mathrm{changed} \gets \mathrm{true}$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return $\mathrm{dom}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

More efficient algorithms exist: the Lengauer--Tarjan algorithm~\cite{lengauer1979} computes dominators in $O(E \log V)$ time.

