\section{Visualization and Analysis}\label{sec:cfg-visualization}

CFGs are visualized using graph drawing tools (Graphviz, DOT) and analyzed using graph algorithms.

\subsection{Graph Drawing}\label{sec:graph-drawing}

CFGs are typically drawn with the entry at the top and edges flowing downward. Back edges (for loops) are drawn as curved arrows going upward.

\subsection{Analysis Algorithms}\label{sec:analysis-algorithms}

Many analyses operate on the CFG:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Reachability.} Which blocks can be reached from the entry?
  \item \textbf{Dominance.} Which blocks dominate which?
  \item \textbf{Loop detection.} Which blocks form natural loops?
  \item \textbf{Cycle detection.} Are there irreducible loops?
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The control-flow graph was introduced by Frances Allen~\cite{allen1970} in her pioneering work on program optimization at IBM. The concepts of dominance and dominance frontiers, developed by Lengauer and Tarjan~\cite{lengauer1979}, became central to SSA construction and optimization. The treatment of exceptional control flow in CFGs was developed in the context of Java and C\# compilers, where exceptions are pervasive. Modern CFG-based analyses underpin virtually all compiler optimizations, from constant propagation to loop-invariant code motion.
\end{historical}

