\section{Advanced CFG Properties and Exceptional Control Flow}\label{sec:cfg-advanced}

\subsection{Reducibility and Irreducibility}\label{sec:reducibility-cfg}

A CFG is \emph{reducible} if its edges can be partitioned into forward edges (forming a DAG) and back edges (going from a node to its dominator). Most structured programs produce reducible CFGs.

\begin{definition}[Reducible CFG]\label{def:reducible}
A CFG is \emph{reducible} if every strongly connected component has a unique entry point (a node that dominates all other nodes in the component). Equivalently, the back edges go only from a node to its dominator.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Irreducible CFG]\label{def:irreducible}
A CFG is \emph{irreducible} if it contains a strongly connected component with multiple entry points. This arises from unstructured control flow (e.g., \texttt{goto} into the middle of a loop).
\end{definition}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Reducible:
  loop:
    x = x + 1;
    if (x > 10) goto done;
    goto loop;
  done:

  // Irreducible:
  // Two entries into the loop body from different sources
  if (cond) goto A; else goto B;
  A: x = 1; goto C;
  B: x = 2; goto C;
  C: x = x + 1;
     if (x > 10) goto done;
     if (cond) goto A; else goto B;  // loop with two entries
  done:
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Natural Loops}\label{sec:natural-loops}

\begin{definition}[Natural Loop]\label{def:natural-loop}
A \emph{natural loop} is the set of nodes that can reach a back edge's target without going through the target. The target is the \emph{header} of the loop and dominates all nodes in the loop.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Natural Loop Detection}\label{alg:natural-loop}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{FindNaturalLoop}{$\mathrm{back\_edge}(n, d)$}
  \State $\mathrm{loop} \gets \{d\}$
  \State $\mathrm{stack} \gets [n]$
  \If{$n \neq d$}
    \State $\mathrm{loop} \gets \mathrm{loop} \cup \{n\}$
  \EndIf
  \While{$\mathrm{stack}$ is not empty}
    \State $m \gets \mathrm{stack}.\mathrm{pop}()$
    \For{each predecessor $p$ of $m$}
      \If{$p \notin \mathrm{loop}$}
        \State $\mathrm{loop} \gets \mathrm{loop} \cup \{p\}$
        \State $\mathrm{stack}.\mathrm{push}(p)$
      \EndIf
    \EndFor
  \EndWhile
  \State \Return $\mathrm{loop}$
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Exceptional Control Flow in Detail}\label{sec:exceptional-detail}

Exceptions create implicit control flow edges that complicate analysis and optimization.

\begin{verbatim}
  // Exception edges in a CFG:
  // B1: x = a / b  (may throw DivisionByZero)
  //     y = f(x)   (may throw any exception)
  //     z = x + 1  (no exception possible)
  //
  // Exception edges:
  // B1 -> Handler (from division)
  // B1 -> Handler (from f(x))
  // B1 -> B2 (normal continuation)
  //
  // The handler block is reached from any instruction that may throw
\end{verbatim}

\begin{definition}[Exceptional Edge]\label{def:exceptional-edge}
An \emph{exceptional edge} is a control flow edge from a block $B$ to an exception handler $H$, representing the possibility that an instruction in $B$ raises an exception that is caught by $H$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Impact of Exceptions on Optimization}\label{sec:exception-optimization}

Exceptional control flow constrains optimizations in several ways:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Code motion.} Instructions that may throw cannot be moved past a catch block boundary.
  \item \textbf{Dead code elimination.} A call that may throw cannot be eliminated even if its result is unused (the side effect of throwing must be preserved).
  \item \textbf{Common subexpression elimination.} Two calls that may throw cannot be merged even if they have the same arguments (each must perform its own exception check).
  \item \textbf{Speculative optimization.} The compiler may speculatively assume that exceptions do not occur, but must insert checks to deoptimize if they do.
\end{itemize}

