\subsection{Splitting a Critical Edge Before Inserting Edge Code}

Instrumentation that belongs to one branch must not run merely
because the source or destination block was visited. An ordinary
edge from a block with multiple successor edges to a block with
multiple predecessor edges is called critical. Inserting code
directly in either block is wrong for such an edge. Split one
specific edge by redirecting it through a fresh block with an
unconditional jump to the original target. In this sketch the
CFG stores unique edge identities, including for parallel edges,
and phi operands, if present, are indexed by incoming edge.
Exceptional edges and edges to special landing pads are excluded.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Critical normal edge $e=(p,q)$ in a valid CFG
\State $m\gets$ fresh empty block
\State replace $e$ in $p$'s terminator with edge $p\to m$
\State $b\gets$ add edge $m\to q$ with unconditional jump
\ForAll{phi nodes $\phi$ at $q$}
  \State $\phi[b]\gets\phi[e]$ \Comment{Forward the original value}
  \State remove $\phi[e]$
\EndFor
\State delete $e$ from the graph and refresh adjacency lists
\State invalidate affected CFG analyses \Comment{Dominance may change}
\State \Return $m$
\end{sudocode}

Let $P$ branch to $J$ on true and $K$ on false, and let $K$ jump
to $J$. The join has a phi choosing 7 from the direct edge
$P\to J$ and 9 from $K\to J$. Place an increment of a counter
for the direct edge in new block $M$. A true branch runs
$P\to M\to J$, increments the counter, and still supplies 7.
A false branch runs $P\to K\to J$ and supplies 9 without changing
the counter. Inserting the increment in $J$ would count both paths.

\Cref{fig:visual-55} shows how the split forwards the phi input as
well as redirecting control through the counter.

